WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.797
AppShelf. A personal demo project, running in Stripe test mode.

2
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.161
This is AppShelf, a small SaaS that sells hosted WordPress sites.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.887
Payments run in Stripe test mode, and every site deletes itself after forty-eight hours.

4
00:00:16.054 --> 00:00:21.525
There are three templates on the shelf: a restaurant, an agency, and a services business.

5
00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:27.397
I'll pick the restaurant and sign up with a throwaway account. There's no email check in the demo.

6
00:00:29.672 --> 00:00:34.298
Choose the template, give the site a title, and pick its address on the network.

7
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.422
Continue to payment hands over to Stripe Checkout.

8
00:00:39.859 --> 00:00:44.989
This is Stripe's own hosted checkout. I pay with the test card, four two four two.

9
00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:51.495
No real money moves: this Stripe account is in test mode.

10
00:00:58.728 --> 00:01:04.401
Back on the dashboard, the payment is confirmed and a background worker builds the WordPress site.

11
00:01:04.558 --> 00:01:07.530
The site is live 8 seconds after the payment.

12
00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:13.957
Here it is: the restaurant template on its own address, ready to edit in WordPress.

13
00:01:15.471 --> 00:01:20.998
Now cancel the plan. The licence turns inactive, and the site is archived right away.

14
00:01:24.324 --> 00:01:27.094
Visitors now see that the site is archived.

15
00:01:28.686 --> 00:01:32.302
Renewing goes through Stripe again, with the same test card.

16
00:01:32.877 --> 00:01:35.589
Same card, four two four two, and pay.

17
00:01:51.018 --> 00:01:53.517
The worker restores the archived site.

18
00:01:53.675 --> 00:01:56.373
7 seconds later, it's live again.

19
00:01:57.961 --> 00:02:01.410
The same site, with the same content, is back online.

20
00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:12.244
It's built with Next.js, Postgres, Stripe and WordPress. The code is on GitHub.
